item.text:
Completed a non-scoring Chapter 6B evidence wave for Joe Biden, United States, 2022.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Selected Health Provisions of the Inflation Reduction Act","publisher":"Congressional Research Service","publication_date":"2022-09-01","url":"https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/IF12203","claim":"Biden signed the Inflation Reduction Act in August 2022; it established Medicare drug-price negotiation, inflation rebates, and other health-cost protections, although major negotiated-price effects were scheduled for later years.","locator":"lines 507–519, especially 507 and 512","provisional_id":"WEB-6B-001","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/IF12203|lines 507-519|IRA established durable Medicare drug-price negotiation and inflation rebates","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["6B"],"methodology_ids":["6B.1","6B.2","6B.3","6B.9"],"source_type":"congressional policy analysis","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Nonpartisan congressional analysis of enacted federal law with precise implementation dates.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Law signed during 2022, but many benefits had prospective rather than realized 2022 effects.","ruler_attribution":"Strong for signing and executive advocacy; enactment also required Congress, and implementation rested with HHS.","contrary_evidence":["Most negotiated-price effects did not improve access during 2022 itself.","The source describes statutory design, not realized household outcomes."],"lenses":["6B.1","6B.2","6B.3","6B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Marketplace 2022 Open Enrollment Period Report: Final National Snapshot","publisher":"Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services","publication_date":"2022-01-27","url":"https://www.cms.gov/newsroom/fact-sheets/marketplace-2022-open-enrollment-period-report-final-national-snapshot","claim":"CMS recorded 14.5 million Marketplace plan selections for 2022, including 3.0 million new consumers, following enhanced subsidies, outreach, and enrollment changes.","locator":"lines 62–63","provisional_id":"WEB-6B-002","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.cms.gov/newsroom/fact-sheets/marketplace-2022-open-enrollment-period-report-final-national-snapshot|lines 62-63|14.5 million 2022 Marketplace plan selections","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["6B"],"methodology_ids":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.5","6B.10"],"source_type":"federal administrative statistics","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct CMS administrative enrollment count with program and period definitions.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 enrollment evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate: Biden supported and administered enhanced access, but enrollment also depended on ARPA legislation, ACA institutions, state marketplaces, and household choices.","contrary_evidence":["Plan selection is not identical to effectuated or continuous coverage.","Enrollment does not establish affordability or access to care after enrollment."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.5","6B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Biennial Health Insurance Survey 2022","publisher":"The Commonwealth Fund","publication_date":"2022-09-29","url":"https://www.commonwealthfund.org/publications/issue-briefs/2022/sep/state-us-health-insurance-2022-biennial-survey","claim":"Despite record coverage gains, 46% of surveyed adults reported delaying or skipping care because of cost, while 49% could not cover an unexpected $1,000 medical bill within 30 days; vulnerability was higher among low-income, Black, and Hispanic adults.","locator":"lines 196–203 and 252–262","provisional_id":"WEB-6B-003","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.commonwealthfund.org/publications/issue-briefs/2022/sep/state-us-health-insurance-2022-biennial-survey|lines 196-203,252-262|persistent medical affordability hardship in 2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["6B"],"methodology_ids":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.8","6B.10"],"source_type":"independent household survey analysis","source_confidence":"medium-high","source_confidence_reason":"Established health-policy research organization using a documented national survey; self-reported hardship and survey uncertainty remain.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Survey conducted and published during 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Low-to-moderate: federal policy affects insurance and costs, but employer plans, providers, states, inherited system design, and household circumstances are major determinants.","contrary_evidence":["The same report attributes record enrollment partly to Biden-era subsidies, outreach, and continuous Medicaid enrollment.","Reported affordability problems do not isolate presidential policy effects."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.8","6B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Poverty Measure That Includes Government Assistance Increased to 12.4% in 2022, When Pandemic Relief Ended","publisher":"U.S. Census Bureau","publication_date":"2023-09-12","url":"https://www.census.gov/library/stories/2023/09/supplemental-poverty-measure.html","claim":"After expanded refundable tax credits and stimulus payments expired, the number of children in Supplemental Poverty Measure poverty increased by more than five million in 2022; taxes and credits removed far fewer people from poverty than in 2021.","locator":"lines 203–215","provisional_id":"WEB-6B-004","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.census.gov/library/stories/2023/09/supplemental-poverty-measure.html|lines 203-215|2022 expiration of relief accompanied child poverty increase over five million","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["6B"],"methodology_ids":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.9","6B.10"],"source_type":"official household survey statistics","source_confidence":"high-with-revision-caveat","source_confidence_reason":"National statistical agency analysis of CPS ASEC data, but Census announced in July 2026 that revised SPM estimates would follow correction of BLS threshold errors.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct calendar-year 2022 outcome, published in 2023.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate and shared: Biden advocated continuation, but extension required Congress; expiration nevertheless describes the federal-policy environment during his rule.","contrary_evidence":["Congress rather than the president alone controlled renewal.","SPM changes also reflected inflation, labor income, other benefits, and measurement choices.","The 2026 threshold correction means exact estimates should be refreshed when revised Census rates appear."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.9","6B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Expansions to Child Tax Credit Contributed to 46% Decline in Child Poverty Since 2020","publisher":"U.S. Census Bureau","publication_date":"2022-09-13","url":"https://www.census.gov/content/census/en/library/stories/2022/09/record-drop-in-child-poverty.html","claim":"Census estimated that the 2021 expanded Child Tax Credit lifted 2.9 million children from poverty and substantially reduced Black and Hispanic child poverty, demonstrating the effectiveness of a Biden-backed welfare intervention immediately preceding 2022.","locator":"lines 163–176 and 193–211","provisional_id":"WEB-6B-005","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.census.gov/content/census/en/library/stories/2022/09/record-drop-in-child-poverty.html|lines 193-211|expanded CTC lifted 2.9 million children from poverty in 2021","disposition":"context","chapter_ids":["6B"],"methodology_ids":["6B.1","6B.3","6B.5","6B.6","6B.8"],"source_type":"official household survey analysis","source_confidence":"high-with-revision-caveat","source_confidence_reason":"Census analysis provides program-specific SPM decomposition; later threshold revisions require caution with exact rates.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Outcome occurred mainly in 2021 but was published in 2022 and establishes the inherited policy baseline whose expiration shaped 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate-to-strong but shared: ARPA was a signature Biden initiative enacted by Congress.","contrary_evidence":["The expansion was temporary and no longer delivered monthly benefits in 2022.","Static SPM decomposition does not estimate behavioral responses."],"lenses":["6B.1","6B.3","6B.5","6B.6","6B.8"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Household Food Security in the United States in 2022","publisher":"U.S. Department of Agriculture, Economic Research Service","publication_date":"2023-10-25","url":"https://ers.usda.gov/publications/107702","claim":"USDA’s nationally representative survey found household food insecurity increased during 2022, including increases among households with children, limiting claims that available assistance fully protected welfare during inflation and benefit normalization.","locator":"report landing page lines 119–124; report ERR-325, Summary and Tables 1A–1B","provisional_id":"WEB-6B-006","canonical_fact_key":"https://ers.usda.gov/publications/107702|ERR-325 Summary and Tables 1A-1B|household food insecurity increased in 2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["6B"],"methodology_ids":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.10"],"source_type":"official nationally representative household survey","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"USDA ERS annual statistical series with documented definitions and survey methodology.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 household outcome, published in 2023.","ruler_attribution":"Low-to-moderate: federal nutrition policy was relevant, but food prices, earnings, state administration, household composition, and expiring congressional relief were also material.","contrary_evidence":["Federal SNAP and emergency allotments continued to support many households.","Food insecurity is not solely determined by presidential policy."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Characteristics of SNAP Households: Fiscal Year 2022","publisher":"USDA Food and Nutrition Service","publication_date":"2025-03-03","url":"https://www.fns.usda.gov/research/snap/characteristics-fy22","claim":"In FY2022, 79% of SNAP households included a child, an elderly person, or a nonelderly disabled person; 75% were at or below poverty, and SNAP plus emergency allotments raised 30% above the poverty line.","locator":"lines 100–102 and Key Findings immediately following","provisional_id":"WEB-6B-007","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.fns.usda.gov/research/snap/characteristics-fy22|Key Findings|SNAP concentrated on vulnerable households and raised 30 percent above poverty","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["6B"],"methodology_ids":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.5","6B.8"],"source_type":"federal program administrative statistics","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"USDA quality-control administrative data with explicit coverage and missing-data limitations.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct FY2022 evidence, though published later.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate for continued federal administration; SNAP is a durable inherited statutory program, and states administer eligibility and delivery.","contrary_evidence":["Emergency allotments were absent from the underlying quality-control benefit amounts unless separately specified.","No FY2021 report was produced because waivers made data incomplete."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.5","6B.8"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Thrifty Food Plan: Better Planning and Accountability Could Help Ensure Quality of Future Reevaluations","publisher":"U.S. Government Accountability Office","publication_date":"2022-12-14","url":"https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-23-105450","claim":"USDA’s reevaluation of the Thrifty Food Plan increased maximum SNAP benefits by 21%, but GAO found that the accelerated process lacked key project-management and quality-assurance elements.","locator":"lines 264–280","provisional_id":"WEB-6B-008","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-23-105450|lines 264-280|21 percent SNAP benefit increase with planning and quality weaknesses","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["6B"],"methodology_ids":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.4","6B.5","6B.6","6B.9"],"source_type":"independent legislative-branch audit","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"GAO audit based on agency records and review of federal program management.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Benefit increase continued into 2022; reevaluation occurred in 2021 and was audited in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate: USDA acted under Biden, but reevaluation was statutorily required and SNAP remained jointly administered with states.","contrary_evidence":["The increase was substantively large and durable despite process weaknesses.","GAO evaluated planning quality, not evidence that benefits were distributed through patronage."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.4","6B.5","6B.6","6B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Emergency Rental Assistance: Treasury’s Oversight Is Limited by Incomplete Data and Risk Assessment","publisher":"U.S. Government Accountability Office","publication_date":"2022-12-20","url":"https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-23-105410","claim":"Emergency Rental Assistance likely helped limit evictions and overwhelmingly reached low-income renters, but Treasury lacked complete payment and recipient data and its reallocations did not consistently align with renter need.","locator":"lines 264–276; report Highlights and What GAO Found","provisional_id":"WEB-6B-009","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-23-105410|Highlights and lines 264-276|ERA limited evictions but had incomplete data and need-alignment weaknesses","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["6B"],"methodology_ids":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.4","6B.5","6B.6","6B.8"],"source_type":"independent legislative-branch audit","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"GAO combined Treasury records, grantee interviews, third-party studies, and eviction data while clearly identifying missing data.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Published in December 2022 and includes reallocations through October 2022, although much recipient analysis concerns 2021 payments.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate: Biden’s Treasury administered ERA2 and reallocations, but Congress created the funding and state, local, territorial, and tribal grantees delivered payments.","contrary_evidence":["Treasury was missing data on a material share of payments.","Local staffing and technology constraints contributed to delays.","Observed eviction changes do not establish a purely federal causal effect."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.4","6B.5","6B.6","6B.8"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Pandemic Unemployment Assistance: Federal Program Supported Contingent Workers Amid Historic Demand, but DOL Should Examine Racial Disparities in Benefit Receipt","publisher":"U.S. Government Accountability Office","publication_date":"2022-06-07","url":"https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-22-104438","claim":"Pandemic Unemployment Assistance extended protection to workers normally ineligible for unemployment insurance, but some applicants waited months and GAO found racial disparities in three of four examined states, including much lower receipt among Black applicants in two states.","locator":"title and publication lines 39–41; Fast Facts and What GAO Found","provisional_id":"WEB-6B-010","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-22-104438|Fast Facts and What GAO Found|PUA supported contingent workers but had delays and racial disparities","disposition":"context","chapter_ids":["6B"],"methodology_ids":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.4","6B.5","6B.6","6B.8"],"source_type":"independent legislative-branch audit","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"GAO analysis of state claimant data, documentation, and claimant experiences, with limited-state scope stated.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Published in 2022 but primarily evaluates an inherited temporary pandemic program and earlier implementation.","ruler_attribution":"Low-to-moderate: federal DOL oversight crossed administrations while state unemployment agencies made eligibility and delivery decisions.","contrary_evidence":["Recipients described the benefits as critical.","Four-state disparity analysis cannot be generalized mechanically to every state."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.4","6B.5","6B.6","6B.8"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Life Expectancy Increases, However Suicides Up in 2022","publisher":"CDC National Center for Health Statistics","publication_date":"2023-11-29","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/pressroom/releases/20231129.html","claim":"U.S. life expectancy rose 1.1 years in 2022, with falling COVID-19 mortality accounting for about 84% of the increase, but it remained below the pre-pandemic level and suicides increased.","locator":"lines 85–88; report findings preceding line 85","provisional_id":"WEB-6B-011","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/pressroom/releases/20231129.html|lines 85-88|2022 life expectancy recovery mainly from lower COVID mortality while suicides rose","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["6B"],"methodology_ids":["6B.5","6B.6","6B.10"],"source_type":"official vital statistics","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"National death-certificate surveillance with explicit provisional-data methods and subsequent final confirmation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 population outcome.","ruler_attribution":"Low-to-moderate: federal vaccination, treatment, and public-health policy contributed, but viral evolution, prior immunity, state policy, health behavior, and demographic conditions were major factors.","contrary_evidence":["Recovery did not erase the 2019–2021 life-expectancy loss.","Suicide deaths and rates increased in 2022."],"lenses":["6B.5","6B.6","6B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Maternal Mortality Rates in the United States, 2022","publisher":"CDC National Center for Health Statistics","publication_date":"2024-05-02","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/data/hestat/maternal-mortality/2022/maternal-mortality-rates-2022.htm","claim":"Maternal mortality fell from 32.9 to 22.3 deaths per 100,000 live births in 2022, but the Black maternal mortality rate remained 49.5—significantly higher than White, Hispanic, and Asian rates.","locator":"lines 48–55 and Figures 1–3, lines 69–85","provisional_id":"WEB-6B-012","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/data/hestat/maternal-mortality/2022/maternal-mortality-rates-2022.htm|lines 48-55|maternal mortality declined but severe Black-white disparity remained","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["6B"],"methodology_ids":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.5","6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"],"source_type":"official vital statistics","source_confidence":"high-with-measurement-caveat","source_confidence_reason":"National vital-statistics files with statistical tests; CDC notes small counts and death-certificate classification issues.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 outcome, published after the period.","ruler_attribution":"Low: federal Medicaid and health policy mattered, but clinical systems, states, demographics, pandemic conditions, and longstanding inequities dominate causal attribution.","contrary_evidence":["The aggregate improvement was substantial.","CDC cautions that annual fluctuation and death-certificate accuracy affect the series."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.5","6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"HUD Releases 2022 Annual Homeless Assessment Report","publisher":"U.S. Department of Housing and Urban Development","publication_date":"2022-12-19","url":"https://archives.hud.gov/news/2022/pr22-253.cfm","claim":"HUD counted 582,462 people experiencing homelessness on one night in January 2022, indicating that substantial housing insecurity remained despite rental assistance and other pandemic protections.","locator":"lines 24 and 37–38; linked 2022 AHAR Part 1 tables","provisional_id":"WEB-6B-013","canonical_fact_key":"https://archives.hud.gov/news/2022/pr22-253.cfm|lines 24,37-38|582462 people experienced homelessness in January 2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["6B"],"methodology_ids":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"],"source_type":"federal administrative census/point-in-time estimate","source_confidence":"medium-high","source_confidence_reason":"Authoritative national HUD series, but point-in-time enumeration undercounts hidden homelessness and 2021 comparability was impaired.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct January 2022 snapshot.","ruler_attribution":"Low: homelessness reflects inherited housing shortages, local policy, rents, labor markets, state and municipal delivery, and federal policy.","contrary_evidence":["HUD compared 2022 primarily with 2020 because 2021 counts were disrupted.","Rental assistance and eviction protections likely prevented worse outcomes."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"COVID-19: Federal Efforts to Provide Vaccines to Racial and Ethnic Groups","publisher":"U.S. Government Accountability Office","publication_date":"2022-02-10","url":"https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-22-105079","claim":"Federal vaccine programs deliberately used demographic data, community outreach, translation, extended hours, and pop-up clinics to reach underserved groups, but available comparisons still showed underrepresentation of some groups and incomplete race data.","locator":"lines 264–293","provisional_id":"E014","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-22-105079|lines 264-293|targeted federal vaccination access with remaining disparities","disposition":"context","chapter_ids":["6B"],"methodology_ids":["6B.3","6B.5","6B.6","6B.8"],"source_type":"independent legislative-branch audit","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"GAO independently reviewed program data, documents, officials, states, stakeholders, and literature.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Published in 2022; underlying program data extend through September 2021.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate: CDC, HRSA, and FEMA programs operated under Biden, while state and local vaccination systems shared responsibility.","contrary_evidence":["Non-Hispanic Black participation remained below population share in the largest program.","Missing race and ethnicity data could explain some or all measured differences."],"lenses":["6B.3","6B.5","6B.6","6B.8"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Vaccination Coverage among Adults in the United States, National Health Interview Survey, 2022","publisher":"Centers for Disease Control and Prevention","publication_date":"2024-05-14","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/adultvaxview/publications-resources/adult-vaccination-coverage-2022.html","claim":"Only 22.8% of adults met the composite age-appropriate vaccination measure in 2022, with lower coverage among Black and Hispanic adults and among people without insurance or a usual source of care.","locator":"lines 62–68 and 112–116, 158–160","provisional_id":"E015","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.cdc.gov/adultvaxview/publications-resources/adult-vaccination-coverage-2022.html|lines 62-68,112-116|low and unequal adult vaccination coverage in 2022","disposition":"context","chapter_ids":["6B"],"methodology_ids":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.5","6B.8","6B.10"],"source_type":"official nationally representative health survey","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"CDC analysis of the National Health Interview Survey with explicit vaccine definitions.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Direct 2022 outcome, published later.","ruler_attribution":"Low: vaccination recommendations and federal programs matter, but insurance, providers, state systems, individual uptake, and longstanding disparities are substantial.","contrary_evidence":["Coverage for some individual vaccines improved or remained stable.","The composite includes several vaccines and should not be interpreted as a COVID-only performance measure."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.5","6B.8","6B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"COVID-19 Vaccination Coverage and Demographic Characteristics of Infants and Children Aged 6 Months–4 Years","publisher":"CDC Morbidity and Mortality Weekly Report","publication_date":"2023-02-17","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/volumes/72/wr/mm7207a4.htm","claim":"By December 31, 2022, only 10.1% of children aged six months to four years had received a first COVID-19 vaccine dose and 5.1% had completed the primary series, with rural, Black, and Hispanic children underrepresented.","locator":"lines 24–46 and 63–93","provisional_id":"E016","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/volumes/72/wr/mm7207a4.htm|lines 24-46,63-93|low and unequal young-child COVID vaccination coverage in 2022","disposition":"context","chapter_ids":["6B"],"methodology_ids":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.5","6B.6","6B.8"],"source_type":"official public-health surveillance","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Near-national jurisdictional administration data with denominators and missing-data limitations disclosed.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Direct June–December 2022 evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Low-to-moderate: FDA authorization and federal distribution were federal responsibilities, but state delivery, clinicians, parental decisions, and access constraints shaped uptake.","contrary_evidence":["Vaccines became available to this group only in June 2022, limiting exposure time.","Race and ethnicity were available for 71.4% of included children."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.5","6B.6","6B.8"]}

## Coverage and synthesis

No chapter score was assigned.

| Lens | Evidence coverage | Residual limitation |
|---|---|---|
| 6B.1 | IRA health reforms, ARPA/CTC legacy, ACA enrollment | Revealed priority is clearer than proof that welfare consistently overrode competing priorities. |
| 6B.2 | Health coverage, affordability, SNAP, food security, rental aid, homelessness | Education, water, sanitation, and service-quality outcomes received thinner direct treatment. |
| 6B.3 | CTC distribution, SNAP caseload, vaccine equity, maternal mortality | Strong evidence of targeted design alongside persistent racial, rural, and income disparities. |
| 6B.4 | GAO audits of SNAP, rental aid, unemployment, and emergency management | No defensible evidence of patronage administration was found; state/local administration complicates federal attribution. |
| 6B.5 | Census, CMS, CDC, USDA measurement and GAO correction findings | Several systems had incomplete recipient or demographic data; Census SPM estimates await a 2026 revision. |
| 6B.6 | Pandemic vaccination, rental aid, nutrition, mortality recovery, expired relief | Crisis outcomes remain confounded by viral evolution, inflation, Congress, states, and inherited programs. |
| 6B.7 | No accepted evidence of benefits conditioned on personal or partisan loyalty | This remains the principal evidence gap: absence of a verified abuse is not affirmative proof that none occurred. |
| 6B.8 | Distributional outcomes in health, vaccination, poverty, nutrition, housing | Persistent medical-cost, maternal-mortality, and vaccination inequities materially qualify favorable evidence. |
| 6B.9 | Durable IRA drug provisions, SNAP baseline revision, ACA institutions | Several 2022 supports were temporary or expired; some IRA effects were prospective. |
| 6B.10 | Coverage gains and mortality recovery versus poverty, food insecurity, affordability, and homelessness | Presidential causal attribution is weak for aggregate life chances and the outcome record is mixed. |

## Research accounting

- Documents discovered: approximately 38 plausible records.
- Underlying documents opened or directly inspected: 18.
- Accepted: 16 source-claim units: 13 newly scoped units and 3 verified reused records.
- Rejected or retained only as discovery leads: 17.
- Access-blocked: 5, including some Congress.gov bill-text routing and an ASPE PDF rendering failure.
- Distinct source organizations: 8 — CRS, CMS, Commonwealth Fund, Census, USDA ERS, USDA FNS, GAO, CDC/NCHS, and HUD when departmental subunits are consolidated.
- Source types: congressional policy analysis, federal administrative statistics, household surveys, vital statistics, public-health surveillance, legislative-branch audits, and housing point-in-time estimates.
- New evidence count: 13.
- Reused evidence count: 3. E014–E016 were reopened and verified; E017 substantially overlaps WEB-6B-003 and was not double-counted. E018 was not needed after stronger direct evidence was retained.
- Duplicate-control note: WEB-6B-003 and prior E017 are one Commonwealth Fund document; WEB-6B-004 and WEB-6B-005 are distinct Census period claims but share the same SPM policy sequence.

A further broad 6B search wave would probably have diminishing value for health, poverty, nutrition, housing, and pandemic response. A narrowly targeted continuation could still add material value for 6B.4 and 6B.7—especially inspector-general, civil-service, benefits-administration, and litigation records concerning political conditionality—and for the relatively thin education, water, sanitation, disability-service, and long-term-care domains.

The read-only workspace prevented updating `research-ledger-manifest.json`. The cumulative parent ledger was not visible through an available filesystem reader, so replacing it with a chapter fragment would have violated the retention requirement. The 16 normalized lines above are therefore the deterministic merge payload; prior entries must remain intact when the parent appends these records.
